Reject a dynamic block whose literal/length table has no end-of-block code.

RFC 1951 3.2.5 ends every block with symbol 256. A dynamic Huffman block
whose code lengths give symbol 256 length 0 describes a block that cannot
end, and zlib's inflate rejects it when the table is read ("invalid code --
missing end-of-block", inflate.c). tinfl did not check, and decoded the
block's literals instead: the input ran out with the stream unfinished,
and whether that was reported as corrupt data or as the caller's output
limit depended on how the input was delivered -- a whole buffer gave a
data error and no output, one-byte chunks gave the limit after as much
output as the limit allowed (zukomp #74).

The check runs once per dynamic block, after the code lengths are read and
before the tables are built, so no byte of such a block is ever emitted.
Fixed blocks always carry a code for 256. Written to be upstreamable as-is.

--- a/miniz.c
+++ b/miniz.c
@@ -2669,6 +2669,15 @@
                         }
                         TINFL_MEMCPY(r->m_code_size_0, r->m_len_codes, r->m_table_sizes[0]);
                         TINFL_MEMCPY(r->m_code_size_1, r->m_len_codes + r->m_table_sizes[0], r->m_table_sizes[1]);
+                        /* RFC 1951 3.2.5: every block ends with symbol 256, so a
+                           literal/length table that gives it no code describes a
+                           block that cannot end. Rejected here, as zlib's inflate
+                           does ("invalid code -- missing end-of-block"), before a
+                           byte of it is decoded. */
+                        if (r->m_code_size_0[256] == 0)
+                        {
+                            TINFL_CR_RETURN_FOREVER(60, TINFL_STATUS_FAILED);
+                        }
                     }
                 }
                 for (;;)
